WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:01.920
Do extra tapes or nondeterminism

2
00:00:02.600 --> 00:00:04.360
make Turing machines more powerful?

3
00:00:05.120 --> 00:00:06.640
They can make descriptions shorter

4
00:00:06.640 --> 00:00:08.820
or computations faster, but the

5
00:00:08.820 --> 00:00:10.940
lecture equivalence theorems preserve the

6
00:00:10.940 --> 00:00:13.080
class of recognized languages. We

7
00:00:13.080 --> 00:00:14.720
will inspect the simulation ideas

8
00:00:14.720 --> 00:00:16.760
and separate expressive power from

9
00:00:16.760 --> 00:00:17.300
running time.

10
00:00:17.820 --> 00:00:20.000
A multitape machine reads one

11
00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:21.760
scanned symbol from each tape

12
00:00:21.760 --> 00:00:23.720
in a transition. Its finite

13
00:00:23.720 --> 00:00:25.900
control chooses new symbols, directions

14
00:00:25.900 --> 00:00:28.200
and state together. The input

15
00:00:28.200 --> 00:00:29.580
begins on the first tape

16
00:00:29.580 --> 00:00:31.120
with the others blank, and

17
00:00:31.120 --> 00:00:32.760
each tape has an independently

18
00:00:32.760 --> 00:00:33.700
positioned head.

19
00:00:34.020 --> 00:00:36.460
Encode the tapes as delimiter-separated

20
00:00:36.460 --> 00:00:38.560
regions on one ordinary tape.

21
00:00:39.120 --> 00:00:40.360
Mark one symbol in each

22
00:00:40.360 --> 00:00:41.980
region to record its virtual

23
00:00:41.980 --> 00:00:44.000
head position. The simulator’s finite

24
00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:45.900
control remembers the tuple of

25
00:00:45.900 --> 00:00:47.480
scanned symbols during a sweep,

26
00:00:47.980 --> 00:00:49.400
then a further sweep performs

27
00:00:49.400 --> 00:00:51.100
the required writes and head-mark

28
00:00:51.100 --> 00:00:51.540
updates.

29
00:00:52.060 --> 00:00:53.380
If a virtual tape needs

30
00:00:53.380 --> 00:00:55.420
an additional cell, shift the

31
00:00:55.420 --> 00:00:57.640
encoding to make space. These

32
00:00:57.640 --> 00:00:59.320
extra sweeps and shifts cost

33
00:00:59.320 --> 00:01:00.760
time, but they do not

34
00:01:00.760 --> 00:01:02.560
change which input words can

35
00:01:02.560 --> 00:01:05.400
eventually be accepted. Thus equivalent

36
00:01:05.400 --> 00:01:07.240
recognition does not mean one

37
00:01:07.240 --> 00:01:09.540
primitive simulated move always takes

38
00:01:09.540 --> 00:01:11.340
one primitive real move.

39
00:01:11.780 --> 00:01:14.000
A nondeterministic machine has a

40
00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:16.380
tree of possible computations. It

41
00:01:16.380 --> 00:01:18.160
accepts if some branch reaches

42
00:01:18.160 --> 00:01:20.300
acceptance, even when other branches

43
00:01:20.300 --> 00:01:22.540
reject or run forever. A

44
00:01:22.540 --> 00:01:24.520
deterministic simulator must search that

45
00:01:24.520 --> 00:01:26.280
tree fairly instead of getting

46
00:01:26.280 --> 00:01:28.480
trapped following one nonhalting branch.

47
00:01:28.940 --> 00:01:31.440
Depth-first search can descend an

48
00:01:31.440 --> 00:01:33.740
infinite path and never inspect

49
00:01:33.740 --> 00:01:36.200
a finite accepting sibling. Breadth-first

50
00:01:36.200 --> 00:01:39.200
exploration, or an equivalent dovetailing

51
00:01:39.200 --> 00:01:41.640
of finite branch addresses, eventually

52
00:01:41.640 --> 00:01:44.160
reaches every finite computation prefix.

53
00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:46.020
Therefore it finds any finite

54
00:01:46.020 --> 00:01:48.360
accepting branch without claiming that

55
00:01:48.360 --> 00:01:49.940
the branches execute for free

56
00:01:49.940 --> 00:01:51.360
in physical parallelism.

57
00:01:51.700 --> 00:01:53.180
For an NTM to be

58
00:01:53.180 --> 00:01:55.300
a decider, all branches must

59
00:01:55.300 --> 00:01:57.440
halt on every input. Its

60
00:01:57.440 --> 00:01:59.640
tree is finitely branching, and

61
00:01:59.640 --> 00:02:01.180
an infinite such tree would

62
00:02:01.180 --> 00:02:03.380
contain an infinite path, so

63
00:02:03.380 --> 00:02:05.580
the all-halting computation tree for

64
00:02:05.580 --> 00:02:07.180
a fixed input is finite.

65
00:02:07.780 --> 00:02:10.000
A fair deterministic simulation can

66
00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:11.900
therefore exhaust it and reject

67
00:02:11.900 --> 00:02:13.200
if none of its branches

68
00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:14.180
accepts.

69
00:02:14.380 --> 00:02:16.360
A doubly infinite tape is

70
00:02:16.360 --> 00:02:18.820
another equivalent variant. Store its

71
00:02:18.820 --> 00:02:20.280
right half on one ordinary

72
00:02:20.280 --> 00:02:21.800
tape and its left half

73
00:02:21.800 --> 00:02:23.260
in reverse order on another,

74
00:02:23.640 --> 00:02:25.620
with finite control remembering which

75
00:02:25.620 --> 00:02:27.640
half is active. Crossing the

76
00:02:27.640 --> 00:02:29.740
origin switches tapes, and directions

77
00:02:29.740 --> 00:02:31.400
reverse while working on the

78
00:02:31.400 --> 00:02:33.180
reversed left-half representation.

79
00:02:33.700 --> 00:02:35.760
The reverse simulation lets a

80
00:02:35.760 --> 00:02:37.640
two-way tape machine reserve a

81
00:02:37.640 --> 00:02:39.360
left-end marker and use only

82
00:02:39.360 --> 00:02:41.160
one side, imitating an ordinary

83
00:02:41.160 --> 00:02:43.680
tape. Together the two simulations

84
00:02:43.680 --> 00:02:46.720
prove equal recognition power. One

85
00:02:46.720 --> 00:02:48.480
inclusion alone would show only

86
00:02:48.480 --> 00:02:49.720
that one model can do

87
00:02:49.720 --> 00:02:50.720
at least as much as

88
00:02:50.720 --> 00:02:51.060
the other.

89
00:02:51.500 --> 00:02:52.880
Things to take note of.

90
00:02:53.300 --> 00:02:55.020
Explain the encoding and head

91
00:02:55.020 --> 00:02:56.940
markers, use fair search for

92
00:02:56.940 --> 00:03:00.020
nondeterminism, and distinguish a recognizer’s

93
00:03:00.020 --> 00:03:01.860
looping branches from a decider’s

94
00:03:01.860 --> 00:03:05.140
all-halting condition. Equivalent computability classes

95
00:03:05.140 --> 00:03:07.440
do not automatically provide equal

96
00:03:07.440 --> 00:03:09.640
runtimes or resolve the efficiency

97
00:03:09.640 --> 00:03:12.080
question behind P versus NP.
